00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.400 align:start position:0%
the government is publishing its  long-awaited plans for a revamped  immigration system to begin at the start  of next year ministers say the  points-based system will shift the  emphasis from low-skilled to  high-skilled workers our political  correspondent Kate McCann is in  Westminster people will be watching this  very closely Kate more details to come  later this evening but what do we know  so far that's right well we're starting  to hear some of the details about what  the new immigration system is going to  look like and really this was always  going to be one of the biggest  challenges for whichever government took  control of brexit because immigration  was such a huge part of that initial  vote to leave the European Union that  Boris Johnson has always been  consistently clear that he wants a  system which is fair for all citizens  from all around the world and that is  what the government is proposing with  its new points-based system workers  would have to earn a certain salary  amount around 25,000 pounds although  there would be exceptions you could earn  20,000 if you were from a particular  profession with the right skills  there will be no low skilled immigration  routes and the government believes that  would exempt around 70% of European  Union citizens from coming to the UK now  clearly their intention here is to take  back control of the immigration system  that's what the Home Secretary herself  has consistently said they also want to  make sure that the UK is getting workers  into the right areas so there are  specific requirements for language for  some of the professions for some of the  qualifications that people will need to  have and they will build up a system of  points which will allow them to move in  to work in the UK now there will be some  exemptions for people like artists  religious leaders there will be some  specific youth schemes and things for  students to encourage exchanges from  countries around the world but there has  been some criticism of these plans there  are some who would have liked to have  seen regional salary differences for  example workers in the north of the  country perhaps paid a little bit less  and some of those in the south that's  not going to happen and they've also  been some concerns from sectors like  Social Care and particularly agriculture  because they believe that some kind of  system like this would mean they  wouldn't be able to have enough workers  the government's message is clear though  they say that the low-skilled  immigration will end  and the UK economy will need to  rebalance itself and start looking to  upskilling British workers to get them  to do some of the jobs that perhaps have  been taken by European Union workers in  the past this will be one of the biggest  challenges for Boris Johnson's  government so tomorrow will be a big day  when we finally see that full system  unveiled  you
 

